    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe spec required rejecting owner creation with **429** once 100+ owners have been registered today (by `registrationDate`).\n\nTo keep structural impact low, I followed the existing pattern in this package (`CityCapacity`, `Namesakes`, etc.) \u2014 small, single-purpose helper classes rather than growing the large `OwnerRestControllerV1` class:\n\n1. **New file `DailyRegistrationLimit.java`** \u2014 a tiny, cohesive helper (one method, `WMC_other = 0`) that counts existing owners with `registrationDate == today` and reports whether the limit of 100 is reached.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 a 3-line early-return branch reusing the `existingOwners` collection the method already fetches:\n   ```java\n   if (DailyRegistrationLimit.isReached(existingOwners)) {\n       return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS);\n   }\n   ```\n\nThis adds the behaviour as a new cohesive unit and keeps the edit to `addOwner` minimal (one branch, no new dependencies), so the change touches only two files with low complexity in each. Seed owners have a `NULL` `registrationDate`, so only owners actually created today count toward the limit \u2014 matching the test's expectation that the first 100 succeed and the 101st returns 429.",
